# SmartSim Per-GPU Supplement

This is a separate, baseline-matched 12-run campaign, not a replacement or an
input to canonical consolidation. The existing 288 canonical runs and all
shared SmartSim ledgers remain untouched.

Matrix: transformer (overlap 0), mlp (overlap 4), placements 96c4g and
96p96c4g, repetitions 1..3. Every cell has 200 timesteps, solution interval
10000, seven inference calls (15/44 warmups; 73/102/131/160/189 measured).
TPQ is 4 only for transformer/96c4g; all other cells use 1. Intra/inter-op
threads are 4/1, explicit DB CPU cores are 0. Four standalone DBs each see one
allocated GPU as logical CUDA device 0, and each model registers one GPU.

Run commands from the repository root using bash. No jobs were submitted by
the implementation or tests.

Offline full matrix (12 commands, no sbatch invocation):

    DRY_RUN=1 bash slurm/submit_cmi_bench_smartsim_pergpu.sh

First two pilots, transformer r1 on both placements, dry run:

    DRY_RUN=1 MODELS=transformer PLACEMENTS='96c4g 96p96c4g' REPS=1 bash slurm/submit_cmi_bench_smartsim_pergpu.sh

Parent/operator-only submission of those pilots, after controller review:

    MODELS=transformer PLACEMENTS='96c4g 96p96c4g' REPS=1 bash slurm/submit_cmi_bench_smartsim_pergpu.sh

MODELS and PLACEMENTS accept whitespace-separated subsets. REPS=N means
repetitions 1..N, where N is 1, 2 or 3. Every invocation creates a unique
campaign. Set FIRST_REP=2 with REPS=3 to submit only repetitions 2 and 3
after the two Transformer r1 pilots pass validation. Every invocation creates a unique
results/cmi_smartsim_pergpu/campaign_<UTC>_<suffix>/ containing manifest.csv,
jobids.csv, commands.sh, source.sha256, source.diff and status/. Dry runs
also preserve their own ledgers, marked DRY_RUN, and cannot validate as runs.
CMI_PERGPU_CAMPAIGN_ROOT optionally redirects those dedicated campaign outputs.
Run artifacts live under scratch/cmi_smartsim_pergpu/; Slurm logs use unique
cell names plus job IDs. The root config_smartsim.toml is never mutated.

Single-node allocation: 1 exclusive c23g node, 96 ranks, 4 GPUs, mem=0, 20 min.
Surrogate heterogeneous allocation exactly follows bench_het_96p96c4g.sbatch:
two c23g components, each 96 ranks, 4 allocated GPUs, mem-per-cpu=5G, 20 min;
group 0 hides its GPUs inside pin_cmi_rank.sh. Group 1 hosts the DBs.
DB steps retain mem=0, ib0 for heterogeneous networking, overlap/exact, solver
wait=0 for heterogeneous runs, a recorded 600-second case timeout and bounded cleanup.
The original two pilots used 300 seconds. Heterogeneous pilot 5109331 completed
its seven calls but exhausted that outer srun budget near teardown; retry 5112134
uses 600 seconds. See results/cmi_smartsim_pergpu/pilot_retry_cells.csv and the
retry campaign's recovery.md. The validator accepts the original implicit
300-second contract or the explicitly recorded new bound, not arbitrary timeouts.

GPU isolation handshake (controller maintained separately): runners export
SMARTSIM_DB_GPU_IDS with four unique actual allocation CVD tokens and
SMARTSIM_DB_GPU_COUNT=4. Single-node runs use inherited CVD; absent CVD uses
an allocation step and GPU UUIDs. Heterogeneous runs always probe group 1:

    srun --het-group=1 --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --cpus-per-task=1 --overlap --exact --mem=0 /bin/bash -c '...'

The probe logs CVD plus GPU index/UUID identity, never CPU-side GPU IDs or an
assumed physical 0..3. Controller entity environment must be applied inside
the Slurm step, since Slurm can reset CVD. Required controller log format:

    SMARTSIM_DB_GPU_MAP {"shard":0,"port":6780,"mask":"GPU-...","logical_device":0}

Exactly four such records must match the endpoint list and GPU-side identity.
The baseline info logging level is preserved. It supplies provider
rank-zero DB-index/rank/leader/endpoint (the logger suppresses nonzero MPI ranks);
other leader ranks are inferred rather than claimed as logged. Model GPU-count=1 is checked in the run-local
configuration and MAIA override. Any additional logged GPU counts must agree.
The existing per-node sampler adds a per-GPU-only
smartsim_gpu_processes.csv (timestamp,pid,gpu_uuid,used_memory,process_command)
at its existing one-second interval. Validation requires a simultaneous sample
of four distinct Redis PIDs on four UUIDs, tied by process command ports to the
four endpoints. Visibility, aggregate memory and a union over time do not pass.
Unreadable /proc process identity fails validation rather than weakening proof.

After pilot jobs finish, use the exact manifest path printed by their submitter:

    python3.11 scripts/validate_cmi_smartsim_pergpu.py --manifest results/cmi_smartsim_pergpu/campaign_<id>/manifest.csv --expected-cells 2 --output results/cmi_smartsim_pergpu/campaign_<id>/validation.json

For the full campaign omit --expected-cells (default 12). Validation reads only
explicit manifest job/cell status files and their matched run paths, verifies
success, resources, seven timings, canonical shared-cell workload equality,
local config, revision/artifact hashes, four endpoints, mapping, provider and
runtime GPU-process evidence. Missing evidence fails closed. It never queries
Slurm, scans for newest/fastest runs, or changes baseline results.

Offline tests:

    python3.11 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_cmi_smartsim_pergpu.py' -v

Python 3.11+ is required for tomllib (also used by the existing runners).

Tests cover 12-cell and 2-pilot dry runs, TPQ, bash syntax, run-local config,
noncontiguous allocated masks, duplicate-mask rejection and fail-closed status,
resource, workload, inference, controller-map and runtime-process checks.
